# CENH3 ChIP-seq Analysis

**From paired-end sequencing reads to candidate CENH3-enriched genomic intervals.**

[Repository home](../README.md) · [Shell script](pipeline.sh)

## Overview

The workflow applies fastp, aligns reads to a T2T assembly with Bowtie2, processes BAM files, and compares CENH3 ChIP-seq with an input control using deepTools. Enriched 5-kb bins are joined into candidate domains.

The supplied [pipeline.sh](pipeline.sh) is an original command template. The configured example below resolves path variables and corrects the sorting order before duplicate marking; it is an adapted example, not a record of changes already applied to the script.

## Requirements

| Software | Role |
| :--- | :--- |
| fastp | Paired-end read quality control |
| Bowtie2 | Reference indexing and read alignment |
| SAMtools | BAM conversion, coordinate sorting, filtering and indexing |
| Sambamba | Duplicate marking |
| deepTools (`bamCompare`) | ChIP/input signal comparison |
| BEDTools | Merging enriched intervals |
| Bash, awk | Workflow execution and filtering |

Use the versions employed in the study and record them with the results; versions were not provided with the original script.

## Inputs

| Input | Description |
| :--- | :--- |
| `genome.fa` | T2T reference assembly |
| `CENH3_R1.fq.gz`, `CENH3_R2.fq.gz` | Paired-end ChIP-seq reads |
| `Input_R1.fq.gz`, `Input_R2.fq.gz` | Matched input-control reads |

Map ChIP and input to the same assembly and process both with the same settings. The example covers one ChIP/input pair; the supplied script does not define replicate handling.

## Before running

| Original detail | Required clarification or adjustment |
| :--- | :--- |
| Variables including `$input_dir`, `$sam`, `$index`, `$output` and `$bed` are undefined | Set explicit paths before execution. `$output` and `$output_dir` must refer to the intended locations. |
| Bowtie2 uses `$fq1` and `$fq2` after fastp | Point alignment at the filtered FASTQs if QC-filtered reads are intended. |
| `samtools sort -n` precedes `sambamba markdup` | Use a coordinate-sorted BAM for this duplicate-marking step. |
| Both fastp and Bowtie2 write `$bn.txt` | Give each step a separate log to prevent overwriting. |
| `bamCompare` writes to `$output_dir`, but the next command reads from `$input_dir` | Use the actual output bedGraph path in the filtering step. |

## Configured command example

Run the following blocks in one Bash session from a dedicated analysis directory. Replace the absolute paths with your own data paths.

### 1. Set paths and build the index

```bash
set -euo pipefail
export LC_ALL=C

genome=/absolute/path/genome.fa
reads=/absolute/path/reads
out="$PWD/cenh3_results"
threads=20
mkdir -p "$out/index" "$out/qc" "$out/bam" "$out/tracks"

bowtie2-build "$genome" "$out/index/genome"
```

### 2. Process ChIP and input libraries

```bash
for sample in CENH3 Input; do
    fastp \
        -i "$reads/${sample}_R1.fq.gz" \
        -I "$reads/${sample}_R2.fq.gz" \
        -o "$out/qc/${sample}_R1.fq.gz" \
        -O "$out/qc/${sample}_R2.fq.gz" \
        --html "$out/qc/${sample}.fastp.html" \
        --json "$out/qc/${sample}.fastp.json" \
        > "$out/qc/${sample}.fastp.log" 2>&1

    bowtie2 -p "$threads" -x "$out/index/genome" \
        -1 "$out/qc/${sample}_R1.fq.gz" \
        -2 "$out/qc/${sample}_R2.fq.gz" \
        -X 2000 --very-sensitive \
        -S "$out/bam/${sample}.sam" \
        2> "$out/bam/${sample}.bowtie2.log"

    samtools view -bh -@ "$threads" "$out/bam/${sample}.sam" \
        | samtools sort -@ "$threads" \
            -o "$out/bam/${sample}.sorted.bam" -

    sambamba markdup -t "$threads" \
        "$out/bam/${sample}.sorted.bam" \
        "$out/bam/${sample}.marked.bam"

    samtools view -F 1804 -f 2 -b -@ "$threads" \
        "$out/bam/${sample}.marked.bam" \
        -o "$out/bam/${sample}.filtered.bam"

    samtools sort -@ "$threads" \
        "$out/bam/${sample}.filtered.bam" \
        -o "$out/bam/${sample}.final.bam"
    samtools index "$out/bam/${sample}.final.bam"
done
```

### 3. Compare signal and merge enriched bins

```bash
bamCompare \
    -b1 "$out/bam/CENH3.final.bam" \
    -b2 "$out/bam/Input.final.bam" \
    -p 12 -bs 5000 \
    --minMappingQuality 20 --ignoreDuplicates \
    --normalizeUsing RPKM --scaleFactorsMethod None \
    --operation log2 \
    -of bedgraph \
    -o "$out/tracks/CENH3_vs_Input.log2.bedgraph"

awk '$4 > 2' "$out/tracks/CENH3_vs_Input.log2.bedgraph" \
    | sort -k1,1 -k2,2n \
    | bedtools merge -i - -d 200000 -c 4 -o mean \
    > "$out/tracks/CENH3_enriched_domains.bed"
```

`--operation log2` makes the original default explicit. This example also adds output sorting and separate QC reports; it has not been run on the study data.

## Key settings

| Setting | Value | Meaning |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| Bowtie2 `-X` | `2000` | Maximum fragment length for concordant paired-end alignment |
| SAMtools `-f` | `2` | Require the properly paired flag |
| SAMtools `-F` | `1804` | Exclude flags for unmapped read/mate, secondary alignment, QC failure and duplicate; supplementary alignments are not explicitly excluded |
| Minimum mapping quality | `20` | Applied during signal generation |
| Bin size | `5000 bp` | Signal resolution |
| Normalization | `RPKM` | Normalize each library before comparison |
| Enrichment threshold | `score > 2` | Threshold on the log₂ comparison, not on raw RPKM |
| Merge distance | `200000 bp` | Join selected bins separated by at most 200 kb |

## Outputs and interpretation

| Output | Description |
| :--- | :--- |
| `qc/*.fastp.*` | QC reports and logs from the adapted example |
| `bam/*.final.bam` and `.bai` | Filtered, coordinate-sorted alignments and indexes |
| `CENH3_vs_Input.log2.bedgraph` | Binned comparison of normalized ChIP and input signal |
| `CENH3_enriched_domains.bed` | Merged intervals; column 4 is the mean of contributing selected-bin scores |

The comparison uses deepTools pseudocount handling. A score above 2 describes a greater-than-four ratio after that handling, rather than an exact raw coverage ratio. The final intervals can include intervening low-signal gaps because of the 200-kb merge distance; their mean scores summarize selected bins, not every base in the full interval.

These are candidate CENH3-enriched domains. The workflow does not include statistical peak calling, replicate concordance analysis, or a validated procedure for exact centromere boundaries. MAPQ filtering can also reduce representation of repetitive centromeric sequence. Report these settings when comparing domain sizes across assemblies.

